select 'thermo-kinetics-equilibrium', 'Gibbs free energy: Overview', 'According to the second law of thermodynamics, for systems reacting at fixed temperature and pressure without input of non-Pressure Volume ( ) work, there is a general natural tendency to achieve a minimum of the Gibbs free energy. A quantitative measure of the favorability of a given reaction under these conditions is the change in Gibbs free energy that is (or would be) caused by the reaction. As a necessary condition for the reaction to occur at constant temperature and pressure, , e.g., electrical) work, which is often equal to zero (in which case work that can be performed as a result of the chemical reaction for the case of a reversible process. If analysis indicates a positive for a reaction, then energy — in the form of electrical or other non- work — would have to be added to the reacting system for as the amount of "free" or "useful" energy available to do non-', 'Wikipedia: Gibbs free energy', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gibbs_free_energy&oldid=1346684211', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'thermo-kinetics-equilibrium' and body = 'According to the second law of thermodynamics, for systems reacting at fixed temperature and pressure without input of non-Pressure Volume ( ) work, there is a general natural tendency to achieve a minimum of the Gibbs free energy. A quantitative measure of the favorability of a given reaction under these conditions is the change in Gibbs free energy that is (or would be) caused by the reaction. As a necessary condition for the reaction to occur at constant temperature and pressure, , e.g., electrical) work, which is often equal to zero (in which case work that can be performed as a result of the chemical reaction for the case of a reversible process. If analysis indicates a positive for a reaction, then energy — in the form of electrical or other non- work — would have to be added to the reacting system for as the amount of "free" or "useful" energy available to do non-');
